'''Sluj''' ([[Sukatic script]]: Ⱄⰾⱆⱜ) was a deified heroic man in [[Scildism]] and one of the [[Corens]]. He is generally considered the first hero of the [[Byrakans]] and of [[Scildism]] itself, although the [[First hero of Scildism#Dispute|latter attribute is disputed]]. Sluj is the protagonist of [[The King who defied the Gods]], an [[wikipedia:epic poetry|epic poem]] written in [[Ottelian language|Ottelian]] by [[Mylas]] in the {{abbr|11th century BCE|9th century BCE (Earth Year Equivalent)}}. He was the legendary king of the [[Nijebe]], a Byrakan tribe, who tried to unite the tribes along the [[Foldava]] against the [[OTHER CIV]], defying gods meddling in human affairs. The Byrakans – and particularly Sluj – are portrayed in a relatively good light, a rare occurrence in Ottelian literature, likely due to Ottelian conflicts with OTHER CIV during Mylas's life. 

Sluj is considered a rallying figure of the [[Qymish people|Qymish nation]], particularly from the {{abbr|21st century|1750 to 1830 (Earth Year Equivalent)}} onwards, when the definition of [[Qymish nationalism]] was the subject of much debate. 

